    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d98">

                <pb facs="0101" n="91" xml:id="pg91"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/985: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-11T16:00:00-05:00">December 11, 1941—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>526. Reference Department’s circular telegram of December 8, 1 a.m. The
                    Ecuadorean Government asserts willingness to comply, is doing what it can but
                    asks for specific suggestions as to how we exercise preventive power,
                    regulations governing telecommunications were published in the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Registro Oficial</hi>, Nos. 478 and 479, of July 3 and
                    4, 1940. The Foreign Minister states that Ecuador will be pleased to modify or
                    amend regulations if we suggest the changes.</p>
                <p>Axis representatives will probably now use clandestine radio: see our
                        No.513.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d98-f1"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
